8. WHY 0.3511/-0.1720 RATHER THAN 0.3636/-0.2293: SCALING vs COVARIATE SET
==============================================================================
observed ratio b_full/b_alone = -0.1720/+0.3511 = -0.4898
target    ratio b_full/b_alone = -0.2293/+0.3636 = -0.6306

Any change of SES units (raw, z-scored, per-2SD, quartile-midpoint, ...) multiplies b_alone and
b_full by the SAME constant, so it leaves the ratio -0.4898 invariant. The target ratio
(-0.6306) differs from the observed ratio by 0.141, so NO linear rescaling of
SES in THIS covariate set can reproduce both quoted numbers simultaneously.
The discrepancy is therefore a MODEL/COVARIATE-SET difference (most plausibly the missing
curriculum and/or separate standardised-test-score covariates named in the dataset description,
and/or a different analytic sample), not a coding choice on my side.

For reference, the two quoted numbers sit on opposite sides of two of my specifications:
  b_alone = 0.3636 ~ my z-scored SES-only logit coefficient          : +0.3511 (diff +0.0125)
  b_full  = -0.2293 ~ my M3 with UNSTANDARDISED SES (per raw unit)   : -0.2316 (diff +0.0023)
  -> consistent with the reference having used a slightly different sample/covariate set
     while measuring the same SES -> BA completion contrast.

